<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cuenca, Spain</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cuenca is a city in Castilla–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, capital of the province of the same name, built on a rocky ridge between the gorges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jucar">Júcar</a> and Huécar rivers. Taken from the Muslims by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1177, it became a bishopric with one of Spain's earliest Gothic cathedrals. Its 'hanging houses' above the gorge are part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuenca-spain.jpg" alt="Cuenca, Spain" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claussmoon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casas_Colgadas_de_Cuenca_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
